1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,530
On Memorial Day, nineteen twenty-five,

2
00:00:02,860 --> 00:00:05,110
at the Rivoli Theater in Times Square,

3
00:00:05,420 --> 00:00:08,300
a group of engineers stood
watching the audience's cardboard

4
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:09,250
fans.

5
00:00:09,660 --> 00:00:11,750
As long as the fans kept fluttering,

6
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:13,870
the experiment had not worked.

7
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:18,150
The cooling system had started late
because of last-minute adjustments,

8
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:19,610
and the room was hot.

9
00:00:20,186 --> 00:00:23,926
Then, as the air cooled, one
patron after another put the fan

10
00:00:23,966 --> 00:00:24,516
away.

11
00:00:24,986 --> 00:00:27,906
The man who had been watching
the fans instead of the film was

12
00:00:27,946 --> 00:00:30,716
Adolph Zukor, the head
of Paramount Pictures.

13
00:00:31,126 --> 00:00:33,056
After the film, in the lobby,

14
00:00:33,246 --> 00:00:35,456
he said that people
were going to like it.

15
00:00:35,991 --> 00:00:39,051
But the machine that cooled that
room had been designed for an

16
00:00:39,091 --> 00:00:40,441
odd first customer.

17
00:00:40,751 --> 00:00:42,661
It was not made for people at all.

18
00:00:42,951 --> 00:00:44,201
It was made for paper.

19
00:00:44,764 --> 00:00:47,054
This is the story of air conditioning,

20
00:00:47,284 --> 00:00:50,604
and how a printing problem in
Brooklyn ended up in nearly every

21
00:00:50,644 --> 00:00:51,174
home.

22
00:00:57,733 --> 00:01:01,613
In nineteen oh one, a young engineer
named Willis Carrier graduated

23
00:01:01,653 --> 00:01:02,623
from Cornell.

24
00:01:03,253 --> 00:01:06,803
In nineteen oh two, he was working
at the Buffalo Forge company,

25
00:01:07,033 --> 00:01:09,083
and he was sent to a
problem in Brooklyn.

26
00:01:09,701 --> 00:01:13,021
A printing plant called Sackett
and Wilhelms was struggling with

27
00:01:13,061 --> 00:01:14,271
humid summer air.

28
00:01:14,781 --> 00:01:17,371
Paper soaked up moisture and swelled.

29
00:01:17,701 --> 00:01:19,941
The colored inks no longer lined up,

30
00:01:19,981 --> 00:01:22,671
and the ink on the pages
would not dry properly.

31
00:01:23,101 --> 00:01:26,601
That caused deadline trouble
for the humor magazine the plant

32
00:01:26,641 --> 00:01:27,391
printed.

33
00:01:27,961 --> 00:01:30,651
Carrier's answer was not
only to cool the air,

34
00:01:30,921 --> 00:01:32,791
but to control how wet it was.

35
00:01:33,221 --> 00:01:35,811
His design is dated the
seventeenth of July,

36
00:01:36,101 --> 00:01:36,971
nineteen oh two.

37
00:01:37,501 --> 00:01:40,921
It aimed to hold the plant at a
steady fifty-five percent humidity

38
00:01:40,961 --> 00:01:44,881
all year round, at about seventy
degrees in winter and eighty

39
00:01:44,921 --> 00:01:45,651
in summer.

40
00:01:46,156 --> 00:01:48,566
The job was big for a young engineer.

41
00:01:49,056 --> 00:01:51,616
The plan called for about
sixty tons of cooling,

42
00:01:51,656 --> 00:01:55,456
which is roughly the effect of
melting a hundred and eight thousand

43
00:01:55,496 --> 00:01:56,766
pounds of ice a day.

44
00:01:57,036 --> 00:02:00,806
The second floor was meant to hold
sixty color printing presses,

45
00:02:00,996 --> 00:02:04,266
and every one of them depended
on paper that did not move.

46
00:02:04,742 --> 00:02:08,122
The machine was built in Buffalo
and installed in the plant that

47
00:02:08,162 --> 00:02:08,792
summer.

48
00:02:09,262 --> 00:02:12,432
Many histories call it the birth
of modern air conditioning.

49
00:02:12,822 --> 00:02:16,782
It cooled the air, but the real
goal was dry paper and straight

50
00:02:16,822 --> 00:02:17,452
colors.

51
00:02:18,216 --> 00:02:20,286
We found no film of
the Brooklyn plant.

52
00:02:20,476 --> 00:02:22,726
One local article adds an irony:

53
00:02:23,116 --> 00:02:25,626
the old building now
houses an art program,

54
00:02:25,776 --> 00:02:28,326
and it says the summer
cooling there is inadequate.

55
00:02:28,916 --> 00:02:31,466
The pictures in this
chapter are stand-ins:

56
00:02:31,716 --> 00:02:34,876
factory scenes from a nineteen
forty-five training film about

57
00:02:34,916 --> 00:02:36,626
heating and air conditioning,

58
00:02:36,956 --> 00:02:39,646
so they show a later
era of the technology,

59
00:02:39,836 --> 00:02:41,266
not the original machine.

60
00:02:47,867 --> 00:02:50,987
For a few years, the idea was
a business secret waiting for a

61
00:02:51,027 --> 00:02:51,457
name.

62
00:02:51,907 --> 00:02:54,297
In nineteen oh six, on
the second of January,

63
00:02:54,527 --> 00:02:57,716
Carrier was granted United States
patent number eight hundred

64
00:02:57,756 --> 00:03:00,077
eight thousand, eight
hundred ninety-seven.

65
00:03:00,247 --> 00:03:02,717
It was called "Apparatus
for Treating Air."

66
00:03:03,207 --> 00:03:04,717
Notice that it says treating,

67
00:03:04,907 --> 00:03:05,677
not cooling.

68
00:03:06,127 --> 00:03:07,687
The goal was to control the air,

69
00:03:07,727 --> 00:03:09,937
and cold was only one tool.

70
00:03:10,471 --> 00:03:13,001
The name we use today
arrived the same year.

71
00:03:13,411 --> 00:03:17,051
In May of nineteen oh six, a
North Carolina textile engineer

72
00:03:17,091 --> 00:03:21,311
named Stuart Cramer used the phrase
"air conditioning" in a speech

73
00:03:21,351 --> 00:03:22,921
to cotton mill owners.

74
00:03:23,171 --> 00:03:25,591
He was talking about
humidity in mills,

75
00:03:25,631 --> 00:03:27,421
not comfort in living rooms.

76
00:03:28,076 --> 00:03:29,746
Factories were the first buyers.

77
00:03:30,216 --> 00:03:33,936
Textile mills, printers and food
makers all needed air that stayed

78
00:03:33,976 --> 00:03:36,346
the same all day, in every season.

79
00:03:36,736 --> 00:03:39,866
Nobody was yet selling cool
air to ordinary people.

80
00:03:40,216 --> 00:03:43,696
In nineteen oh seven, one early
order was a silk weaving plant

81
00:03:43,736 --> 00:03:46,646
system, delivered for about
thirty-one hundred dollars.

82
00:03:47,216 --> 00:03:51,146
Textile makers cared about
humidity just as printers did.

83
00:03:51,618 --> 00:03:54,748
In nineteen fifteen, on
the twenty-sixth of June,

84
00:03:54,978 --> 00:03:58,308
Carrier and six other engineers
set up their own firm,

85
00:03:58,638 --> 00:04:00,338
the Carrier Engineering Corporation,

86
00:04:00,378 --> 00:04:03,788
with thirty-two thousand six
hundred dollars in capital.

87
00:04:04,274 --> 00:04:06,244
Then, in nineteen twenty-two,

88
00:04:06,534 --> 00:04:09,764
the company introduced its
first centrifugal chiller.

89
00:04:10,114 --> 00:04:13,594
It is widely described as the
machine that made cooling very

90
00:04:13,634 --> 00:04:17,474
large spaces practical, though
the full engineering story is

91
00:04:17,514 --> 00:04:19,704
more complicated than one invention.

92
00:04:20,194 --> 00:04:22,444
It was unveiled in May of that year.

93
00:04:22,850 --> 00:04:25,560
We found no film of the
early Carrier machines.

94
00:04:26,150 --> 00:04:28,660
The pictures in this
chapter are stand-ins:

95
00:04:28,990 --> 00:04:31,570
Washington photographs from
the Library of Congress,

96
00:04:31,610 --> 00:04:35,430
showing air conditioning machinery
and the rooftop work at a

97
00:04:35,470 --> 00:04:39,070
hotel, from roughly the nineteen
twenties to the nineteen forties.

98
00:04:39,110 --> 00:04:42,720
The exact dates of those
photographs are not certain.

99
00:04:49,267 --> 00:04:51,517
So who would pay for
cool air for people?

100
00:04:51,847 --> 00:04:53,677
The answer was the movie theatre.

101
00:04:54,087 --> 00:04:57,387
Summer had always been the season
when theatres lost their crowds,

102
00:04:57,427 --> 00:05:01,657
because a hall full of people
in July is a very hot place.

103
00:05:02,067 --> 00:05:05,617
One account says movie houses
simply closed in the summer.

104
00:05:06,180 --> 00:05:08,910
The Rivoli in nineteen
twenty-five was the test.

105
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:11,550
The system started late, as we heard,

106
00:05:11,680 --> 00:05:14,200
but once the room cooled,
the fans went away,

107
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:16,130
and the idea was proven.

108
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:18,590
Cooling also helped the studios.

109
00:05:18,820 --> 00:05:22,180
The new talking pictures needed
doors and windows kept shut,

110
00:05:22,220 --> 00:05:26,090
and cool air kept actors
comfortable under the bright lights.

111
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:29,680
By nineteen thirty, more than
three hundred theatres had hung

112
00:05:29,720 --> 00:05:33,890
banners from their marquees that
read "Cooled by Refrigeration."

113
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:37,070
A cool seat became a
reason to buy a ticket.

114
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:40,910
For the first time, people were
paying not for a film alone,

115
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,090
but for the weather inside the room.

116
00:05:43,797 --> 00:05:45,597
This was air conditioning for a crowd,

117
00:05:45,637 --> 00:05:47,487
and it was a public treat.

118
00:05:47,857 --> 00:05:49,887
Almost no one had it at home yet.

119
00:05:50,217 --> 00:05:53,377
To get there, the machine had to
shrink from a plant in a basement

120
00:05:53,417 --> 00:05:55,527
to a box that fit in a window.

121
00:05:55,985 --> 00:05:58,695
We found no film of the
Rivoli on that night.

122
00:05:59,185 --> 00:06:00,865
The pictures here are stand-ins:

123
00:06:00,905 --> 00:06:04,805
nineteen twenties to nineteen forties
photographs of air conditioning

124
00:06:04,845 --> 00:06:07,395
and lighting at a Washington theatre,

125
00:06:07,505 --> 00:06:11,255
and an air conditioning display
from a Washington electric company.

126
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:21,070
Home units took longer, because
they were too big and too costly.

127
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:23,890
In nineteen thirty-two, two inventors,

128
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:26,530
Schultz and Sherman, put
a window unit on sale.

129
00:06:26,840 --> 00:06:30,430
It was very expensive, but
sources disagree on how much.

130
00:06:30,860 --> 00:06:33,590
One gives a price in the tens
of thousands of dollars.

131
00:06:34,020 --> 00:06:37,053
Another says single-room units
cost as little as four hundred

132
00:06:37,093 --> 00:06:39,170
dollars in the nineteen thirties.

133
00:06:39,340 --> 00:06:42,620
We could not settle it, and
either figure was far beyond most

134
00:06:42,660 --> 00:06:43,390
families.

135
00:06:43,930 --> 00:06:47,740
Later, a man named Henry Galson
is credited with a smaller,

136
00:06:47,950 --> 00:06:49,180
cheaper window unit.

137
00:06:49,590 --> 00:06:52,810
One source says he sold more than
forty-three thousand of them

138
00:06:52,850 --> 00:06:54,640
by nineteen forty-seven.

139
00:06:55,359 --> 00:06:58,599
By the nineteen sixties, companies
such as Westinghouse were

140
00:06:58,639 --> 00:07:00,279
selling window units to families,

141
00:07:00,319 --> 00:07:02,969
in colors and wood-grain finishes.

142
00:07:03,499 --> 00:07:07,019
The color film in this chapter
is a Westinghouse sales film,

143
00:07:07,059 --> 00:07:08,659
from about nineteen sixty-six,

144
00:07:08,699 --> 00:07:13,289
so it is an advertisement, not
a neutral record of the time.

145
00:07:13,712 --> 00:07:17,712
In nineteen fifty-four, an experiment
called the Austin Air-Conditioned

146
00:07:17,752 --> 00:07:21,112
Village built twenty-two homes
with cooling designed in from

147
00:07:21,152 --> 00:07:21,902
the start.

148
00:07:22,292 --> 00:07:25,722
Central air was becoming something
a house could simply have.

149
00:07:26,052 --> 00:07:27,302
In nineteen seventy-five,

150
00:07:27,712 --> 00:07:31,222
about forty-six out of every
hundred new homes had it.

151
00:07:31,472 --> 00:07:34,262
By two thousand five, that
number was eighty-nine.

152
00:07:35,148 --> 00:07:37,198
The numbers climbed
through the century.

153
00:07:37,568 --> 00:07:38,798
In nineteen ninety-three,

154
00:07:39,208 --> 00:07:43,228
the same agency counted about
sixty-six million American households

155
00:07:43,268 --> 00:07:44,678
with air conditioning.

156
00:07:45,188 --> 00:07:47,908
Among homes built in
the nineteen seventies,

157
00:07:47,948 --> 00:07:50,838
about half had central
air in the early surveys,

158
00:07:51,048 --> 00:07:53,938
and about two thirds had it
by two thousand fifteen.

159
00:07:54,717 --> 00:07:57,957
By two thousand fifteen, the
United States government's energy

160
00:07:57,997 --> 00:08:02,277
agency reported that eighty-seven
percent of American homes used

161
00:08:02,317 --> 00:08:03,327
air conditioning.

162
00:08:03,647 --> 00:08:07,107
About sixty percent used
only central systems,

163
00:08:07,337 --> 00:08:10,767
and about twenty-three percent
used only individual units,

164
00:08:10,957 --> 00:08:12,267
such as window units.

165
00:08:12,737 --> 00:08:14,987
A little under five percent used both.

166
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:18,170
A young engineer set out
to fix a printing plant,

167
00:08:18,340 --> 00:08:20,990
and what he built was a way of
controlling the weather indoors.

168
00:08:21,700 --> 00:08:24,550
A theatre in Times Square
cooled a summer crowd.

169
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:27,670
A window unit shrank until
a family could buy one.

170
00:08:27,880 --> 00:08:31,340
And a machine that began by
keeping paper straight now hums in

171
00:08:31,380 --> 00:08:34,560
nearly every home, and most of
us never think about it until

172
00:08:34,600 --> 00:08:35,630
the day it stops,

173
00:08:35,980 --> 00:08:38,470
and the room goes
still and warm again,

174
00:08:38,620 --> 00:08:40,930
like that theatre before
the fans were put away.

175
00:08:41,867 --> 00:08:42,757
Ordinary objects.

176
00:08:42,947 --> 00:08:44,277
Extraordinary stories.
